WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:16.069
"The sir!"

2
00:00:16.829 --> 00:00:19.651
Peterson had the face of a man dazed with astonishment.

3
00:00:20.332 --> 00:00:20.792
"What of it?

4
00:00:21.332 --> 00:00:24.434
Did the bird return to life and flap through the kitchen window?"

5
00:00:25.055 --> 00:00:29.177
Holmes leaned over the back of the sofa to get a better view of the man's excited face.

6
00:00:29.197 --> 00:00:30.518
"See here, sir!

7
00:00:30.899 --> 00:00:32.900
Look what my wife found in the bird's gullet!"

8
00:00:33.356 --> 00:00:37.937
He held out his hand, opening it to reveal a brilliant blue stone.

9
00:00:38.898 --> 00:00:45.019
Slightly smaller than a bean, it was so pure and radiant that it twinkled like a star in

10
00:00:45.039 --> 00:00:46.139
the center of his palm.

11
00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:48.680
Sherlock Holmes got up and whistled.

12
00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:52.021
"Peterson!" he said.

13
00:00:52.041 --> 00:00:54.362
"That's buried treasure indeed.

14
00:00:55.062 --> 00:00:56.802
Do you know what you've got?"

15
00:00:56.822 --> 00:00:58.563
"A diamond, sir?

16
00:00:58.683 --> 00:01:00.043
Some kind of precious stone?"

17
00:01:00.063 --> 00:01:02.304
"It's more than a precious stone.

18
00:01:02.984 --> 00:01:05.165
It is the precious stone."

19
00:01:05.185 --> 00:01:08.687
"Not the Countess of Morcar's Blue Carbuncle!"

20
00:01:09.127 --> 00:01:09.727
I exclaimed.

21
00:01:10.407 --> 00:01:11.888
"Precisely," said Holmes.

22
00:01:11.908 --> 00:01:14.569
"I ought to know its size and shape by now.

23
00:01:15.249 --> 00:01:19.051
I've been reading about the theft of the stone in the newspaper every day.

24
00:01:19.731 --> 00:01:23.553
It's absolutely unique, and its value can only be guessed.

25
00:01:24.173 --> 00:01:30.036
The reward is a thousand pounds, but the market price must be more than twenty times that much."

26
00:01:30.792 --> 00:01:32.153
"A thousand pounds!

27
00:01:32.213 --> 00:01:33.094
Good Lord!"

28
00:01:33.855 --> 00:01:37.578
Peterson plopped down in a chair and stared at the two of us.

29
00:01:37.598 --> 00:01:44.845
"That's just the reward," Holmes said, "but I happen to know that the stone's sentimental value would make

30
00:01:44.885 --> 00:01:47.867
the countess part with half her fortune to get it back."

31
00:01:47.887 --> 00:01:52.992
"It was lost, if I remember correctly, at the Hotel Cosmopolitan," I said.

32
00:01:53.012 --> 00:01:57.496
"Precisely━on December 22, just five days ago.

33
00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:03.141
A plumber was charged with taking the Blue Carbuncle from the lady's jewel case, which was in her

34
00:02:03.181 --> 00:02:03.802
dressing room.

35
00:02:04.542 --> 00:02:06.922
The evidence against him seems to be strong.

36
00:02:07.663 --> 00:02:09.763
There's an article about it here, I believe."

37
00:02:10.644 --> 00:02:17.205
Holmes rummaged through his newspapers, glancing over the dates, until at last he smoothed one page out, doubled

38
00:02:17.245 --> 00:02:22.447
it over, and read the following paragraph: "‘Hotel Cosmopolitan Jewel Robbery.

39
00:02:23.227 --> 00:02:30.662
John Horner, 26, plumber, was charged with removing the valuable gem known as the Blue Carbuncle from the

40
00:02:30.702 --> 00:02:32.923
jewel case of the Countess of Morcar.

41
00:02:33.663 --> 00:02:38.226
James Ryder, the hotel manager, stated he had shown Horner to the dressing room on the day of

42
00:02:38.266 --> 00:02:41.327
the robbery in order that he might repair a heating grate.

43
00:02:41.887 --> 00:02:45.409
He remained with Horner a little while but was soon called away.

44
00:02:46.149 --> 00:02:49.931
On returning, Ryder found that the plumber had disappeared.

45
00:02:50.591 --> 00:02:56.039
The bureau had been forced open and the small leather box where the countess had kept her jewel

46
00:02:56.319 --> 00:02:58.301
was lying empty on the dressing table.

47
00:02:59.142 --> 00:03:05.287
Ryder called the police and Horner was arrested the same evening, but the stone could not be found

48
00:03:05.467 --> 00:03:08.189
either on his person or in his rooms.

49
00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:15.336
Catherine Cusack, the maid, said she heard Ryder's cry when he discovered the theft, and she rushed into

50
00:03:15.376 --> 00:03:15.796
the room.

51
00:03:16.637 --> 00:03:20.200
There she found everything to be as the hotel manager described it.

52
00:03:20.992 --> 00:03:28.659
Inspector Bradstreet described Horner's arrest, saying, "He struggled frantically and protested his innocence in the strongest terms."

53
00:03:29.399 --> 00:03:35.365
Because the prisoner had a previous conviction for robbery, the magistrate referred the matter to a higher court.

54
00:03:36.245 --> 00:03:38.888
Horner was intensely emotional during the proceedings.

55
00:03:39.708 --> 00:03:43.452
He fainted at the conclusion and was carried out of the court.'"

56
00:03:44.112 --> 00:03:45.533
Holmes tossed the paper aside.

57
00:03:45.553 --> 00:03:48.156
"So much for the news account," he said.

58
00:03:48.768 --> 00:03:54.111
"What possible sequence of events could lead from an empty jewel case to the gullet of a goose?

59
00:03:55.092 --> 00:03:59.654
You see, Watson, our little deductions have suddenly become much more important.

60
00:04:00.354 --> 00:04:05.877
Here is the stone; the stone came from the goose, and the goose came from Mr.

61
00:04:05.917 --> 00:04:11.260
Henry Baker, the gentleman with the bad hat and all the other characteristics I bored you with."

62
00:04:12.141 --> 00:04:13.662
Holmes stood up and started to pace.

63
00:04:13.682 --> 00:04:19.658
"We must find this gentleman and learn what part he played in this mystery.

64
00:04:20.398 --> 00:04:24.301
Let's start with something simple━an advertisement in all the evening papers.

65
00:04:25.142 --> 00:04:27.224
If that fails, we'll try something else."

66
00:04:27.924 --> 00:04:29.206
"What will you say in the ad?"

67
00:04:29.526 --> 00:04:29.926
I asked.

68
00:04:29.946 --> 00:04:33.949
"Give me a pencil and that slip of paper, my dear Watson," said Holmes.

69
00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:40.595
Then he began to write: "A black felt hat and a goose were found on Tottenham Court Road.

70
00:04:41.416 --> 00:04:49.033
Mr. Henry Baker may claim these items by applying at 6:30 this evening at 221B Baker Street."

71
00:04:49.694 --> 00:04:50.454
"But will he see it?"

72
00:04:50.774 --> 00:04:51.155
I asked.

73
00:04:51.715 --> 00:04:55.157
"He is sure to keep an eye on the papers," said Holmes.

74
00:04:55.177 --> 00:04:58.138
"To a poor man, the loss was a heavy one.

75
00:04:58.859 --> 00:05:03.942
He was so frightened by his bad luck in breaking the window and Peterson's approach that his only

76
00:05:04.042 --> 00:05:05.242
thought was to run away.

77
00:05:06.003 --> 00:05:09.305
But since then he must bitterly regret dropping the bird.

78
00:05:10.145 --> 00:05:14.928
Using his name is a good idea because everyone he knows will call his attention to it.

79
00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:19.763
Here you are, Peterson, run down and put this advertisement in the evening papers."

80
00:05:19.783 --> 00:05:21.224
"Which papers, sir?"

81
00:05:21.244 --> 00:05:26.087
"Oh, the Globe, Star, Evening News, Standard all of them!"

82
00:05:26.107 --> 00:05:27.588
"Very well, sir.

83
00:05:28.088 --> 00:05:28.769
And this stone?"

84
00:05:28.789 --> 00:05:29.910
"Ah yes.

85
00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:32.852
I'll keep the stone," Holmes said.

86
00:05:32.872 --> 00:05:33.872
"Thank you.

87
00:05:34.253 --> 00:05:38.095
And Peterson, buy a goose on the way back and leave it with me.

88
00:05:38.896 --> 00:05:42.958
We must have one to give in place of the one your family is now devouring."

89
00:05:43.659 --> 00:05:47.618
When the security guard left, Holmes lifted the stone in front of the light.

90
00:05:47.638 --> 00:05:49.760
"Beautiful," he said.

91
00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:52.182
"See how it glints and sparkles.

92
00:05:53.043 --> 00:05:55.144
Of course it's at the center of a crime.

93
00:05:55.785 --> 00:05:57.046
Every good stone is.

94
00:05:57.827 --> 00:06:00.429
Precious gems are such a favorite temptation.

95
00:06:01.150 --> 00:06:06.014
With the larger and older jewels, every facet may stand for a bloody deed."

96
00:06:06.734 --> 00:06:08.996
Holmes inspected the gem under his magnifying glass.

97
00:06:09.016 --> 00:06:14.977
"This stone was found on an island off the coast of southern China, and though it has all

98
00:06:15.037 --> 00:06:19.101
the characteristics of a garnet, it is blue instead of deep red.

99
00:06:19.982 --> 00:06:25.047
Not yet twenty years old, this beautiful gem already has a sinister history.

100
00:06:25.787 --> 00:06:33.054
This piece of crystallized carbon has caused two murders, a fistfight, a suicide, and several robberies.

101
00:06:34.055 --> 00:06:38.880
Who'd believe such a pretty trinket could lead to prison and the hangman's noose?

102
00:06:39.832 --> 00:06:43.255
I'll lock it up in my safe now and drop a line to the countess to say that

103
00:06:43.275 --> 00:06:43.735
we have it."

104
00:06:44.416 --> 00:06:46.578
"Do you think the plumber, Horner, is innocent?"

105
00:06:47.058 --> 00:06:47.458
I asked.

106
00:06:47.478 --> 00:06:48.699
"I can't tell."

107
00:06:48.719 --> 00:06:51.021
"Could Henry Baker have anything to do with it?"

108
00:06:51.461 --> 00:06:52.002
I persisted.

109
00:06:52.022 --> 00:06:58.627
"It's more likely, I think, that Henry Baker had no idea the bird he carried was worth more

110
00:06:58.687 --> 00:06:59.948
than its weight in gold.

111
00:07:00.849 --> 00:07:06.574
However, I'll know for sure by a very simple test, if he answers our advertisement."

112
00:07:07.334 --> 00:07:08.095
"And in the meantime?"

113
00:07:08.775 --> 00:07:10.328
"I can do nothing," Holmes said.

114
00:07:10.348 --> 00:07:14.451
"In that case I'll be on my way, but I'll be back tonight.

115
00:07:14.991 --> 00:07:16.312
I'd like to see what happens next."

116
00:07:16.332 --> 00:07:18.693
"Tonight then," Holmes said.

117
00:07:18.713 --> 00:07:20.554
"I dine at seven.

118
00:07:21.115 --> 00:07:23.116
Roasted chicken is on the menu, I believe.

119
00:07:23.936 --> 00:07:26.798
Perhaps I ought to ask the cook to examine its gullet."

120
00:07:27.798 --> 00:07:30.520
It was a little past six when I returned to Baker Street.

121
00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:36.503
As I approached the house, I saw a tall man in a knitted cap with a coat buttoned

122
00:07:36.563 --> 00:07:40.058
up to his chin waiting outside in the light of the entryway.

123
00:07:40.799 --> 00:07:42.760
Just as I arrived, the door opened.

124
00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:48.885
"Mr. Henry Baker, I believe," Holmes said, greeting the man in a friendly manner.

125
00:07:48.905 --> 00:07:52.488
"Please come in and take this chair by the fire."

126
00:07:53.389 --> 00:07:59.274
As I followed the visitor into the house, Holmes said, "Ah, Watson, you've come at just the right

127
00:07:59.374 --> 00:08:03.637
time." Once we were inside, Holmes turned to his new guest.

128
00:08:04.398 --> 00:08:06.579
"Is this your hat, Mr.

129
00:08:06.620 --> 00:08:08.014
Baker?" he asked.
